<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hvalsey Church</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hvalsey Church is a ruined stone church of the Norse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaqortoq">Qaqortoq</a>, and the best-preserved Norse building in Greenland. A wedding held there in September 1408, recorded in Icelandic documents, is the last written record of the Norse Greenlanders. The ruins are part of the Kujataa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hvalsey-church.jpg" alt="Hvalsey Church" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Number 57 at English Wikipedia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hvalsey_Church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the sagas, the land around Hvalsey was claimed by Thorkell Farserk, a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, and the farm's dwelling stands on an earlier building of Erik's time that may have been Thorkell's home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> around the year 1000, and churches followed. The stone church is thought to date from the early 14th century, though archaeological finds hint that an older church stood on the site. Late medieval documents list it among the 10 to 14 parish churches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-settlement">Eastern Settlement</a>, and Ívar Bárðarson, the Norwegian bishop's emissary to Garðar in the late 14th century, describes the homestead as a royal manor called Þjóðhildarstaðir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The church stands on a narrow strip of land at the head of a fjord, about 70 metres from the water, within a typical Norse farmstead. The main farm building covered about 1,300 square metres, with eleven rooms combining living quarters, a banqueting hall and livestock pens; there were further pens, a horse enclosure for visitors, a storehouse up the hill and a warehouse at the shore.</p><p>The church measures about 16 by 8 metres and follows the Anglo-Norwegian style of the early 13th century. It held some 30 to 35 people, and a dyke marked the edge of the churchyard. Its walls, about 1.5 metres thick, are of carefully fitted granite fieldstones, some weighing 4 to 5 tonnes, and mortar made from crushed shells would have made it white; the name of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaqortoq">Qaqortoq</a>, the white place, may come from it. The roof was probably of turf on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. Window openings widen inwards, a feature unknown in Icelandic churches but known in early churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 14 or 16 September 1408 the church hosted the wedding of Thorstein Olafsson and Sigrid Björnsdóttir, who later settled in Thorstein's native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> legend tells of a war in which the Inuk leader K'aissape attacked Hvalsey and burned the Norse in their houses, but archaeologists have found no sign of a fire. Because it was built over graves that were never removed, the church's foundations sank and it has partly collapsed, yet it has withstood the weather better than other Norse buildings. The site is now part of a sheep farm.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hvalsey Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Western Settlement was abandoned in the mid-14th century, and the last written record from the Eastern Settlement is a wedding at <b>Hvalsey</b> church in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/state/norse-greenland">Norse Greenland</a></li></ul></section>
